package Libraries.Compute.Hardware

use Libraries.Containers.Array
use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray
use Libraries.Containers.Number64BitArray

/*
The HardwareCompute class runs a program on the graphics card (a compute
shader) over a block of numbers, whatever the graphics system underneath:
Libraries.Compute.Hardware.Vulkan.VulkanCompute today, and WebGPU later
for the web. Programs that use it only talk to this class.

A computation is made from the program's compute device, which the
system gives (GameStateManager:GetComputeDevice): the running game's
graphics card when a game is drawing with the same graphics system, or one
with no window (headless). Every computation shares that device. Then:

    1. SetProgram: the shader's source, in the language the graphics
       system takes (GLSL for Vulkan, WGSL for WebGPU).
    2. SetData: the numbers. The program reads and writes them in place.
    3. Run: run the program over a grid of work groups and wait; then
       GetValues reads the numbers back.

Numbers can be given two ways:

    Number32BitArray   32-bit numbers, copied as they are: for graphics, or
                       work where 32 bits are precise enough.
    Number64BitArray   Quorum's own 64-bit numbers, held on the graphics
                       card as the number format says (SetNumberFormat):
        DOUBLE_NUMBER_32  (the default) each number as two 32-bit
                          numbers, high and low, about 48 bits. Works on
                          every graphics card, Apple's included, which
                          have no 64-bit numbers. Numbers past the range of
                          a 32-bit number (above about 3.4e38, or below
                          about 1e-38) can't be held this way.
        NUMBER_64         each number as it is, 64 bits, exactly as Quorum
                          holds it. Storing them works on every graphics
                          card; the program decides how to compute with
                          them: GLSL double on cards with 64-bit numbers,
                          or IEEE 754 arithmetic emulated with integers on
                          any card, which gives the CPU's answers bit for
                          bit.

The program must be written for the format: with DOUBLE_NUMBER_32, number
i is 32-bit numbers 2i (high) and 2i + 1 (low) of the buffer, and
Libraries.Compute.Hardware.Shaders.DoubleNumber32ShaderCode reads and
writes them; with NUMBER_64, number i is 64-bit number i, and
Libraries.Compute.Hardware.Shaders.Number64ShaderCode reads, writes and
computes with them.

Several runs can also be sent together, in order, each seeing what the
one before it wrote: BeginRuns, then AddRun for each, then FinishRuns,
which sends them all at once and waits. Each run can carry a few integers
of its own (SetRunIntegerCount), so one program can work on a different
part of the data each time, such as each step of a chain of matrix
multiplies kept on the graphics card.

Dispose releases what the computation made; the device stays.

Attribute: Example

use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray

// doubles every number it is given
text program = "#version 450
layout (std430, set=0, binding=0) buffer Numbers { float data[]; } numbers;
layout (local_size_x = 1) in;
void main() { numbers.data[gl_GlobalInvocationID.x] *= 2.0; }
"
GameStateManager manager
HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
if device not= undefined
    HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
    Number32BitArray values
    values:SetSize(3)
    values:Set(0, 1)
    values:Set(1, 2)
    values:Set(2, 3)
    compute:SetProgram("Doubler", program)
    compute:SetData(values)
    compute:Run(3, 1, 1)
    compute:GetValues(0, values)
    output values:Get(2)
    compute:Dispose()
end
*/
class HardwareCompute
    /* Each 64-bit number as two 32-bit numbers, high and low: every graphics card. */
    public constant integer DOUBLE_NUMBER_32 = 0
    /* Each 64-bit number as it is, 64 bits: every graphics card (see Number64ShaderCode for computing with it). */
    public constant integer NUMBER_64 = 1

    integer numberFormat = DOUBLE_NUMBER_32

    /*
    This action returns the device this computation runs on: the one it
    was made from (HardwareComputeDevice:CreateCompute).

    Attribute: Returns the device.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        output compute:GetDevice():GetSystemName()
    end
    */
    blueprint action GetDevice returns HardwareComputeDevice

    /*
    This action sets how Quorum's 64-bit numbers are held on the graphics
    card: DOUBLE_NUMBER_32 (the default) or NUMBER_64 (each number as it
    is). Both work on every graphics card. Set it before SetData, and give
    a program written for the same format.

    Attribute: Parameter format DOUBLE_NUMBER_32 or NUMBER_64.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareCompute compute = manager:GetComputeDevice():CreateCompute()
    compute:SetNumberFormat(compute:DOUBLE_NUMBER_32)
    */
    action SetNumberFormat(integer format)
        if format not= DOUBLE_NUMBER_32 and format not= NUMBER_64
            alert("The number format needs to be DOUBLE_NUMBER_32 or NUMBER_64, not " + format + ".")
        end
        numberFormat = format
    end

    /*
    This action returns how Quorum's 64-bit numbers are held on the
    graphics card.

    Attribute: Returns DOUBLE_NUMBER_32 or NUMBER_64.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareCompute compute = manager:GetComputeDevice():CreateCompute()
    if compute:GetNumberFormat() = compute:DOUBLE_NUMBER_32
        output "Each number is held as two 32-bit numbers."
    end
    */
    action GetNumberFormat returns integer
        return numberFormat
    end

    /*
    This action sets the program to run. Setting the same name and source
    again keeps the compiled program, so it costs nothing; a different one
    is compiled.

    Attribute: Parameter name a name for the program, used in error messages.

    Attribute: Parameter code the program's source, in the graphics system's shader language (GLSL for Vulkan).

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    text program = "#version 450
layout (std430, set=0, binding=0) buffer Numbers { float data[]; } numbers;
layout (local_size_x = 1) in;
void main() { numbers.data[gl_GlobalInvocationID.x] *= 2.0; }
"
    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        compute:SetProgram("Doubler", program)
    end
    */
    blueprint action SetProgram(text name, text code)

    /*
    This action copies 32-bit numbers to the graphics card for the program
    to read and write, as they are. The space on the card is kept between
    calls while the numbers fit.

    Attribute: Parameter data the numbers.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number32BitArray values
        values:SetSize(2)
        values:Set(0, 1.5)
        values:Set(1, 2.5)
        compute:SetData(values)
    end
    */
    blueprint action SetData(Number32BitArray data)

    /*
    This action copies Quorum's 64-bit numbers to the graphics card for the
    program to read and write, held as the number format says. With
    DOUBLE_NUMBER_32, each number becomes two 32-bit numbers, so the card
    holds twice as many.

    Attribute: Parameter data the numbers.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Number64BitArray

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number64BitArray values
        values:SetSize(2)
        values:Set(0, 1.000000001)
        values:Set(1, 2.000000001)
        compute:SetData(values)
    end
    */
    blueprint action SetData(Number64BitArray data)

    /*
    This action runs the program over x by y by z work groups (each as
    large as the program says) and waits for it to finish.

    Attribute: Parameter x the number of work groups across.

    Attribute: Parameter y the number of work groups down.

    Attribute: Parameter z the number of work groups deep.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray

    text program = "#version 450
layout (std430, set=0, binding=0) buffer Numbers { float data[]; } numbers;
layout (local_size_x = 1) in;
void main() { numbers.data[gl_GlobalInvocationID.x] *= 2.0; }
"
    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number32BitArray values
        values:SetSize(100)
        compute:SetProgram("Doubler", program)
        compute:SetData(values)
        compute:Run(100, 1, 1)
    end
    */
    blueprint action Run(integer x, integer y, integer z)

    /*
    This action sets how many integers each run is given, for the program
    to read as a block of uints (in Vulkan, a push_constant block). They
    let one program work on a different part of the data each run. 0, the
    default, gives none. Set it before the program first runs; changing it
    later builds the program's pipeline again.

    Attribute: Parameter count how many integers each run is given.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareCompute compute = manager:GetComputeDevice():CreateCompute()
    compute:SetRunIntegerCount(2)
    */
    blueprint action SetRunIntegerCount(integer count)

    /*
    This action starts a group of runs. Add each with AddRun, then send
    them all with FinishRuns.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Array
    use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray

    // adds the run's integer to every number
    text program = "#version 450
layout (std430, set=0, binding=0) buffer Numbers { float data[]; } numbers;
layout (push_constant) uniform Settings { uint amount; } settings;
layout (local_size_x = 1) in;
void main() { numbers.data[gl_GlobalInvocationID.x] += float(settings.amount); }
"
    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number32BitArray values
        values:SetSize(10)
        compute:SetRunIntegerCount(1)
        compute:SetProgram("Adder", program)
        compute:SetData(values)
        compute:BeginRuns()
        Array<integer> first
        first:Add(1)
        compute:AddRun(10, 1, 1, first)
        Array<integer> second
        second:Add(2)
        compute:AddRun(10, 1, 1, second)
        compute:FinishRuns()
        // every number is now 3
    end
    */
    blueprint action BeginRuns

    /*
    This action adds a run to the group started by BeginRuns. It starts
    after the run before it has finished, and sees everything that run
    wrote.

    Attribute: Parameter x the number of work groups across.

    Attribute: Parameter y the number of work groups down.

    Attribute: Parameter z the number of work groups deep.

    Attribute: Parameter runIntegers this run's integers: as many as SetRunIntegerCount said, or undefined when that is 0.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray

    text program = "#version 450
layout (std430, set=0, binding=0) buffer Numbers { float data[]; } numbers;
layout (local_size_x = 1) in;
void main() { numbers.data[gl_GlobalInvocationID.x] *= 2.0; }
"
    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number32BitArray values
        values:SetSize(10)
        compute:SetProgram("Doubler", program)
        compute:SetData(values)
        compute:BeginRuns()
        compute:AddRun(10, 1, 1, undefined)
        compute:AddRun(10, 1, 1, undefined)
        compute:FinishRuns()
    end
    */
    blueprint action AddRun(integer x, integer y, integer z, Array<integer> runIntegers)

    /*
    This action sends the runs added since BeginRuns to the graphics card,
    all at once, and waits for the last to finish.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray

    text program = "#version 450
layout (std430, set=0, binding=0) buffer Numbers { float data[]; } numbers;
layout (local_size_x = 1) in;
void main() { numbers.data[gl_GlobalInvocationID.x] *= 2.0; }
"
    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number32BitArray values
        values:SetSize(10)
        compute:SetProgram("Doubler", program)
        compute:SetData(values)
        compute:BeginRuns()
        compute:AddRun(10, 1, 1, undefined)
        compute:FinishRuns()
    end
    */
    blueprint action FinishRuns

    /*
    This action forgets the compiled program, so the next run compiles it
    and builds its pipeline again. It is for measuring what compiling
    costs; programs don't need it.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        compute:ForgetProgram()
    end
    */
    blueprint action ForgetProgram

    /*
    This action returns how long the last compile took: turning the
    program's source into the graphics system's own form.

    Attribute: Returns the time in milliseconds.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareCompute compute = manager:GetComputeDevice():CreateCompute()
    output "Compiling took " + compute:GetCompileTime() + " ms"
    */
    blueprint action GetCompileTime returns number

    /*
    This action returns how long building the last pipeline took: where
    the graphics driver prepares the compiled program for its card.

    Attribute: Returns the time in milliseconds.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareCompute compute = manager:GetComputeDevice():CreateCompute()
    output "The pipeline took " + compute:GetPipelineTime() + " ms"
    */
    blueprint action GetPipelineTime returns number

    /*
    This action returns one 32-bit number from the graphics card, after a
    run.

    Attribute: Parameter index which 32-bit number of the data, from 0.

    Attribute: Returns the number.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number32BitArray values
        values:SetSize(1)
        values:Set(0, 4)
        compute:SetData(values)
        output compute:GetValue(0)
    end
    */
    blueprint action GetValue(integer index) returns number

    /*
    This action copies 32-bit numbers back from the graphics card, after a
    run: as many as into holds, starting at 32-bit number start, in one
    copy.

    Attribute: Parameter start the first 32-bit number to copy, from 0.

    Attribute: Parameter into where the numbers go; its size says how many.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number32BitArray values
        values:SetSize(4)
        compute:SetData(values)
        Number32BitArray lastTwo
        lastTwo:SetSize(2)
        compute:GetValues(2, lastTwo)
    end
    */
    blueprint action GetValues(integer start, Number32BitArray into)

    /*
    This action copies Quorum's 64-bit numbers back from the graphics card,
    after a run, read as the number format says: as many as into holds,
    starting at number start. With DOUBLE_NUMBER_32, number start is
    32-bit numbers 2 * start and 2 * start + 1, added together.

    Attribute: Parameter start the first number to copy, from 0, counted in 64-bit numbers.

    Attribute: Parameter into where the numbers go; its size says how many.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute
    use Libraries.Containers.Number64BitArray

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        Number64BitArray values
        values:SetSize(4)
        compute:SetData(values)
        Number64BitArray answer
        answer:SetSize(4)
        compute:GetValues(0, answer)
    end
    */
    blueprint action GetValues(integer start, Number64BitArray into)

    /*
    This action sets a file to keep compiled programs in between program
    runs, so a later run can skip the graphics driver's compile. "" (the
    default) keeps nothing. A graphics system without such a cache ignores
    it.

    Attribute: Parameter path the file's path; it is made if it doesn't exist.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareCompute compute = manager:GetComputeDevice():CreateCompute()
    compute:SetProgramCacheFile("Data/programs.cache")
    */
    blueprint action SetProgramCacheFile(text path)

    /*
    This action releases what the compute made on the graphics card: its
    program, data and commands. A running game's device is left alone.

    Attribute: Example

    use Libraries.Game.GameStateManager
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareComputeDevice
    use Libraries.Compute.Hardware.HardwareCompute

    GameStateManager manager
    HardwareComputeDevice device = manager:GetComputeDevice()
    if device not= undefined
        HardwareCompute compute = device:CreateCompute()
        compute:Dispose()
    end
    */
    blueprint action Dispose
end
